+# Prospective spatial Wilson-loop area route — block36
+
+The root supplied the area-selection plus uniform-analyticity candidate. This contract is frozen before new finite selection checks or coefficient calculations. The primary papers math-ph/0411042 and math-ph/0412040 have been opened; the latter's marked-observable polymer weight suggests a possible common complex disk and exponential support prefactor. That suggestion is not yet declared proved.
+
+Target: normalized fundamental trace around a planar R by S rectangle on a fixed cubic lattice. Derive exact link-center selection for every perturbative coefficient and prove any finite Z3 plaquette chain filling the rectangle has at least RS insertions. Resolve repeated orientations, off-plane surfaces, and torus wraparound. Fixed prospective finite controls: rectangles1x1,1x2,2x2 in sufficiently padded periodic boxes; retain an adverse torus rectangle exceeding half its projected torus area, where the complementary sheet can be smaller.
+
+Hard routeA: use the marked-polymer estimates in math-ph/0412040 Section2, not weak analyticity alone, to obtain a common complex-u disk and |F_A(u)|<=C0^|support A| uniformly in finite volume. The paper's periodic boundary convention and range-wide onsite condition must be matched explicitly; rescaling the grouped onsite sum is allowed only if stated. A cluster-defined holomorphic continuation may replace a uniformly isolated complex Riesz branch, but it must match the real normalized ground expectation and the finite Riesz germ. Never conjugate complex u in the bra.
+
+Hard routeB: if the published marked-polymer weight cannot supply the support-uniform bound, record that exact residual rather than publish Taylor selection as an area law. Ordinary gap/exponential clustering or real-axis boundedness alone are not sufficient substitutes.
+
+No repo writes. No physical confinement, static-charge potential, temporal Wilson rectangle, arbitrary coupling or continuum claim. New imports and periodic/infinite-family scope must remain explicit.
