+# Independent cold review of root marked-observable proof
+
+Target ROOT_DERIVATION.md SHA3d2d869494e39a844d904e664aaf20605c2420bab3d562d3b7f57a8ded7ee031, reviewed after own proof SHA721889e7 was frozen. Result: PASS on the mathematical argument, with two optional sentence-level precision improvements below. Root's proposed route was exposed before both derivations; this is a cold proof check, not a claim of independent discovery.
+
+The exact four-cell classical-block mapping is necessary and correct: each cell electric term is repeated four times, restoring aK, while the perturbation norm is 3|u|. Infinite onsite dimension causes no new counting factor, since the expansion uses binary excitation projections and operator norm bounds. The marked support must mean the marked connected polymer from the factorization construction: its ordinary connected pieces each attach to the inserted connected S, so adjoining S gives a connected support. A generic arbitrary set merely containing S would not be connected. The subsequent spanning-tree count applies to the correctly defined marked polymer and gives a geometry-only exponential base, including the torus family.
+
+The weighted KP arithmetic checks directly. rho=|w|e^n and a=n leave q=ce²epsilon≤1/4, incompatible sum at most n0/3. Pinning an external root does not require a small root fugacity. Multiplying the root by e^n0 and the ordinary pieces by their reserve gives a bound on the sum of absolute cluster weights times e^(total support count). Summing root sizes yields ||A||(ce²)^m/(1−q)≤||A||(2ce²)^m for m≥1. This is stronger tail reserve than own proof's half-weight choice; both are consistent because constants and thresholds are existential. Large time-boundary or wrapping clusters must have correspondingly large support, giving normal convergence with fixed-range conversion.
+
+The complex sandwich has the right parameter on both sides. The bounded-perturbation inclusion–exclusion expansion is holomorphic and its absolute bounds control the same disk; the ordinary cluster logarithm gives a nonzero partition function. Differentiating an insertion I+lambda A at lambda=0, or the equivalent one-mark cancellation identity, yields exactly one root without treating its full weight as a small ordinary gas activity. Time limits, not finite-time quotients, coincide with the rank-one Riesz expectation near zero. In the paragraph beginning 'The preceding weighted absolute bound', the phrase 'the complex expression equals Tr(A P(u))' is read as its just-defined time limit F_A; explicitly saying 'the time limit' would remove possible ambiguity. There is no need to assert complex spectral isolation throughout the uniform disk.
+
+The F3 all-order rule and periodic half-area condition agree with the independently reviewed primary proof. Real normalized trace uses the two orientation sectors; complex normalized trace uses one, and the same upper bound holds for each. The higher-order Schwarz lemma produces the bound without a 1/(1−u/u0) loss. No confinement, positive lower bound, or numerical threshold is inferred. The proof genuinely supplies the extra uniform complex control that finite Taylor selection alone lacks.
